            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Sorts a set of points - positions, poses, feature vectors of any length - into k groups of points that lie close together, and gives which group each point is in and the centre of each group. k-means clustering.\n\nUse it to find where people tend to gather on a floor, to build a small vocabulary of recurring poses or gestures from a recording (each centre a typical pose), to divide a point cloud into objects, or to quantize a continuous feature into a few states.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.kmeans <k: int>\nDefault: k 3.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\npoints:\nThe points, as rows of coordinates (a plain list is points on a line). Every input is grouped and sent. Rows with nan are left out.\n\nk (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many groups.\n\nfollow:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default), each new set starts from the last set's centres, so group 0 stays the same group from one frame to the next as the points move. Off, every set is grouped afresh, and the numbers can swap.\n\nseed:\nAn option, under the node. -1 (the default) picks the starting centres differently each run; any other number repeats the same grouping.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nlabels:\nWhich group each point is in: 0 to k - 1.\n\ncentres:\nThe centre of each group, one row each. Sent first, then the labels.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nSix points: three near 0 0 and three near 5 5. With k 2 the labels are three of one number and three of the other, and the centres are about 0.1 0.13 and 5 5. A metro re-sends the points twice a second; the labels stay put because follow is on.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.kdtree finds which of a fixed set of points is nearest. pc_cluster_filter groups the points of a point cloud. scipy.label finds regions in a grid.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Sorts a set of points - positions, poses, feature vectors of any length - into k groups of points that lie close together, and gives which group each point is in and the centre of each group. k-means clustering.\n\nUse it to find where people tend to gather on a floor, to build a small vocabulary of recurring poses or gestures from a recording (each centre a typical pose), to divide a point cloud into objects, or to quantize a continuous feature into a few states.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.kmeans <k: int>\nDefault: k 3.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\npoints:\nThe points, as rows of coordinates (a plain list is points on a line). Every input is grouped and sent. Rows with nan are left out.\n\nk (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many groups.\n\nfollow:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default), each new set starts from the last set's centres, so group 0 stays the same group from one frame to the next as the points move. Off, every set is grouped afresh, and the numbers can swap.\n\nseed:\nAn option, under the node. -1 (the default) picks the starting centres differently each run; any other number repeats the same grouping.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nlabels:\nWhich group each point is in: 0 to k - 1.\n\ncentres:\nThe centre of each group, one row each. Sent first, then the labels.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nSix points: three near 0 0 and three near 5 5. With k 2 the labels are three of one number and three of the other, and the centres are about 0.1 0.13 and 5 5. A metro re-sends the points twice a second; the labels stay put because follow is on.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.kdtree finds which of a fixed set of points is nearest. pc_cluster_filter groups the points of a point cloud. scipy.label finds regions in a grid."
        },
